WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:07.232
of cool wind rush through a wide valley.

2
00:00:07.933 --> 00:00:11.995
A herd of cattle ahead of him, a cowboy rides steadily across the plains.

3
00:00:12.656 --> 00:00:19.300
He sits astride his horse in a wide-brimmed hat, a fringed shirt, jeans, and pointed cowboy boots, enjoying

4
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:19.941
the sunset.

5
00:00:20.641 --> 00:00:22.883
Soon he will arrive at the ranch he calls home.

6
00:00:23.763 --> 00:00:28.827
This is the classic image of the cowboy—a vision of strength and bravery that has left its mark

7
00:00:28.887 --> 00:00:30.188
on the Western imagination.

8
00:00:31.048 --> 00:00:37.071
The cowboys portrayed on television and the movie screen, however, are quite different from the original cowboys of

9
00:00:37.111 --> 00:00:37.871
the Wild West.

10
00:00:38.832 --> 00:00:44.034
Cowboys, in the traditional sense, are people who tend to the animals on a farm and drive them

11
00:00:44.094 --> 00:00:46.035
across large tracts of open land.

12
00:00:46.715 --> 00:00:53.198
They use lassos, horses, and dogs to herd livestock for ranchers, rounding up a few animals at the

13
00:00:53.278 --> 00:00:57.640
end of the day or guiding thousands of head of cattle miles across the country.

14
00:00:58.564 --> 00:01:04.387
On these great "cattle drives," cowboys lead the herds to the cattle markets where they will be sold.

15
00:01:04.807 --> 00:01:10.549
Cowboys are also trained to take care of other animals, such as sheep, pigs, and chickens, and perform

16
00:01:10.589 --> 00:01:12.050
many tasks around the farm.

17
00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:19.253
Societies all over the world have people who take care of animals, but the modern cowboy descends from

18
00:01:19.293 --> 00:01:21.774
the Spanish tradition of the sixteenth century.

19
00:01:22.534 --> 00:01:28.757
Much of Spain is dry and mountainous, with sparse vegetation, so cattle needed to roam widely to get

20
00:01:28.817 --> 00:01:29.497
enough to eat.

21
00:01:30.197 --> 00:01:33.678
The Spanish cowboys tended these wide-ranging herds on horseback.

22
00:01:34.338 --> 00:01:40.600
When the Spanish conquistadors came to the New World, they brought horses, which had been extinct in the

23
00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:43.841
Western Hemisphere since the end of the last ice age.

24
00:01:44.541 --> 00:01:50.442
The horses aided the Spanish in the settlement of New Spain, which later became Mexico and the southwestern

25
00:01:50.502 --> 00:01:51.223
United States.

26
00:01:52.123 --> 00:02:01.142
The Mexican-American War (1846–1848) expanded the United States' territory into Mexico and helped to bring the cowboy way

27
00:02:01.182 --> 00:02:03.343
of life northward into the United States.

28
00:02:04.404 --> 00:02:12.507
After the American Civil War, which lasted from 1861 to 1865, the cowboy tradition continued to travel north

29
00:02:12.587 --> 00:02:13.267
into Canada.

30
00:02:14.268 --> 00:02:19.590
Although history books rarely mention female cowboys, known as "cowgirls," they did exist.

31
00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:23.772
Many women worked on ranches, doing every possible task.

32
00:02:24.448 --> 00:02:29.890
They even ran family operations, which was unusual in other sectors of society for the time.

33
00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:37.012
Split skirts, which resemble loose pants, were invented so that women were not hampered by their clothing, especially

34
00:02:37.092 --> 00:02:37.732
on horseback.

35
00:02:38.713 --> 00:02:45.035
But since riding astride was considered unladylike, many women chose to use sidesaddles that had been adapted for

36
00:02:45.115 --> 00:02:49.236
ranch work and allowed women to ride while wearing conventional female attire.

37
00:02:50.468 --> 00:02:55.350
Advances in technology brought the cowboy era to a close at the end of the nineteenth century.

38
00:02:56.170 --> 00:03:02.593
Barbed wire was invented in the 1870s and used to construct inexpensive fences around farms and pasture.

39
00:03:03.453 --> 00:03:08.775
The wire helped ranchers control their cattle, with fewer men on horseback needed to watch the herds.

40
00:03:09.676 --> 00:03:16.478
Unfortunately, the ranchers overgrazed their land, leading to widespread starvation of their herds in the decade that followed.

41
00:03:17.339 --> 00:03:22.882
The expansion of railroads in the late 1890s and the invention of the cattle truck in the 1940s

42
00:03:23.383 --> 00:03:28.187
made long cattle drives across the nation—and many cowboys—largely unnecessary.

43
00:03:29.708 --> 00:03:36.454
As work on farms became scarcer, cowboys and cowgirls turned to other ways of making a living; many

44
00:03:36.494 --> 00:03:38.096
began to compete in rodeos.

45
00:03:38.916 --> 00:03:43.340
The word "rodeo" comes from a Spanish word for the process of rounding up cattle.

46
00:03:44.181 --> 00:03:50.353
In the 1880s, a sport grew out of this practice, incorporating influences from Spanish bullfights.

47
00:03:51.253 --> 00:03:57.095
The events that make up a rodeo have changed little since that time and include trick roping, calf

48
00:03:57.135 --> 00:03:59.316
roping, and bull riding, among others.

49
00:04:00.176 --> 00:04:07.298
The Rodeo Association of America was formed partly to organize the many rodeo competitions across the country, but

50
00:04:07.398 --> 00:04:12.120
also to create a point system to fairly judge participants' performances.

51
00:04:13.344 --> 00:04:20.428
Cowgirls began to increasingly participate in rodeos in the early 1900s, when the Wild West show, a large,

52
00:04:20.508 --> 00:04:22.149
traveling rodeo, became popular.

53
00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:26.472
However, fewer events were organized for women than for men.

54
00:04:27.212 --> 00:04:33.976
Several organizations, such as the National Cowgirl Museum and Hall of Fame and the National Women's Rodeo Association,

55
00:04:34.537 --> 00:04:39.500
now exist to promote cowgirls and recognize the accomplishments of the women of the American West.

56
00:04:40.796 --> 00:04:43.438
The number of cowboys and cowgirls is declining.

57
00:04:44.119 --> 00:04:46.721
Our fascination with them, however, continues to grow.

58
00:04:47.461 --> 00:04:51.525
Popular culture has heavily romanticized the cowboy's solitary lifestyle.

59
00:04:52.165 --> 00:04:58.850
Cowboys are often featured in books and movies, particularly those from the 1930s to the 1960s, when the

60
00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:01.453
genre known as the "western" was in its heyday.

61
00:05:02.253 --> 00:05:05.576
These portrayed the cowboy as a seminomadic outlaw.

62
00:05:06.408 --> 00:05:12.032
As a result, many people believe the stereotype that cowboys are irresponsible and violent loners.

63
00:05:12.833 --> 00:05:18.256
The term "cowboy" is often used pejoratively to describe someone who has no respect for authority and an

64
00:05:18.336 --> 00:05:20.038
inclination toward lawlessness.

65
00:05:20.938 --> 00:05:27.303
But there are qualities that cowboys are admired for as well: their self-reliance, courage, and willingness to work

66
00:05:27.363 --> 00:05:34.708
hard. For this reason, the cowboy has become a widely accepted symbol of American individualism and independence.

67
00:05:35.516 --> 00:05:40.940
The National Football League honored all cowboys when they created a new team called the Cowboys in Irving,

68
00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:42.882
Texas, a suburb of Dallas.

69
00:05:44.162 --> 00:05:49.666
The cowboy's style of dress has also been incorporated into popular culture, with a few changes.

70
00:05:50.527 --> 00:05:53.509
Traditionally, cowboys did not wear fancy clothing.

71
00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:58.153
They were often paid very little and wore whatever hand-me-downs they could find.

72
00:05:58.913 --> 00:06:05.229
Wide-brimmed cowboy hats, which are now internationally popular, were worn merely to shield their eyes from the sun,

73
00:06:05.689 --> 00:06:09.391
and pointed boots with heels were worn to make it easier for their feet to stay in the

74
00:06:09.431 --> 00:06:16.053
stirrups. People now wear cowboy hats and boots with everything from skirts and jeans to business attire.

75
00:06:17.154 --> 00:06:26.558
As of 2003, there were 9,730 working cowboys in the United States, of which 3,290 were rodeo workers.

76
00:06:27.438 --> 00:06:31.980
The duties of these modern cowboys have changed dramatically since the sixteenth century.

77
00:06:32.732 --> 00:06:39.097
They now control many different types of farm equipment, including tractors and other vehicles designed for agricultural work,

78
00:06:39.738 --> 00:06:43.381
but much of their time is still spent on horseback, riding on the plains.

79
00:06:44.182 --> 00:06:49.446
Today's rodeo cowboys still perform their acts in the style of the cowboys on the Spanish frontier.

80
00:06:50.206 --> 00:06:55.291
The cowboy way of life has seen many changes, but as long as there are farms and rodeos,

81
00:06:55.491 --> 00:06:56.211
it will endure.
